from pathlib import Path
p=Path('research/quantum-build-60-2026-10-15-owner/complex/items/thm-boundary-maximum-modulus-principle.md');s=p.read_text();s=s.replace('The complex-plane topology and Euclidean-plane topology agree ([[rem-complex-plane-euclidean-dictionary]]).','The domain is nonempty, open and connected in $d_{\\mathbb C}$ ([[def-complex-domain]], [[def-complex-metric-convergence-and-continuity]]). Use the coordinate isometric homeomorphism of [[thm-complex-coordinate-isometry-and-topology-transport]].')
a=s.index('1.1 The closure');b=s.index('\n2.1',a)
s=s[:a]+r'''1.1 Choose $c\in\mathbb C$ and $R>0$ with $\Omega\subseteq B(c,R)$, using boundedness ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]], [[def-metric-ball]]). The closure is closed by [[thm-metric-closure-characterisation]] and is nonempty since it contains $\Omega$. If $|x-c|>R$, the ball of radius $(|x-c|-R)/2$ around $x$ misses $\Omega$: the reverse triangle inequality ([[lem-metric-reverse-triangle]]) gives $|y-c|>R$ for its points. Thus every closure point has $|x-c|\le R$, and the closure lies in $B(c,R+1)$. The coordinate theorem transfers closedness and boundedness to $\mathbb R^2$, so [L2] gives compactness there and transport gives compactness back. The modulus laws of [[lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws]] give $||u|-|v||\le|u-v|$; together with continuity of $f$ in [[def-metric-continuity]] they show $|f|$ is continuous. By [L3] it has a maximizer $z_0\in\overline\Omega$. [given, L2, L3, choose, algebra]
''' +s[b:]
a=s.index('3.1 If');b=s.index('\n4.1',a)
s=s[:a]+r'''3.1 If $z_0\notin\Omega$, then $z_0\in\partial\Omega$ because $\Omega$ is open. Otherwise step 2.1 gives $f=c_0$ on $\Omega$. For $x\in\overline\Omega$ and any $\varepsilon>0$, continuity gives a ball in the restricted closure on which $|f(y)-f(x)|<\varepsilon$; adherence gives $y\in\Omega$ in that ball, so $|c_0-f(x)|<\varepsilon$ and $f(x)=c_0$. Finally the boundary is nonempty. If empty, openness and $\partial\Omega=\overline\Omega\setminus\Omega$ give $\Omega=\overline\Omega$, a nonempty clopen set. Connectedness of the plane, transferred from [L4], implies $\Omega=\mathbb C$. But for every ball $B(c,R)$, [[cor-archimedean-reciprocal]] supplies a positive natural $n>R+|c|$, and $|n-c|\ge n-|c|>R$; hence the plane is unbounded. This contradicts boundedness. Any boundary point therefore has modulus equal to the maximum in the constant branch. [given, step 1.1, step 2.1, L4, choose, algebra]
''' +s[b:];p.write_text(s)
